## Statement

Prove that for every integer $n$, the number $n^3-n$ is divisible by $6$.

## Hint

Factor $n^3-n$.

## Solution

We have $n^3-n=(n-1)\,n\,(n+1)$, a product of three consecutive integers.

- Among three consecutive integers, one is a multiple of $3$.
- Among them, at least one is even.

So the product is divisible by $2$ and by $3$. Since $2$ and $3$ are coprime, it is divisible by $6$.
